Description:
    DEGEmbedR enables statistical hypothesis testing of gene–function relationships 
    using embedding representations derived from large language models (LLMs). The 
    package provides the main `RunDEGEmbedR()` function to perform nonparametric
    statistical testing ofgene–function cosine similarity distributions between
    differentially expressed genes (DEGs) and build-in or customized background genes. 
    DEGEmbedR is designed specifically for human genes and supports a curated background of ~18k human 
    protein-coding genes from the NCBI Gene database. All built-in annotations and 
    similarity matrices are human-specific. The framework supports curated functional 
    collections such as MSigDB GO Biological Processes (BP) and MSigDB Canonical Pathways (CP). 
    In addition, the `GSAI` mode generates AI-derived functional hypotheses from 
    the input DEGs and then evaluates the similarity between the de novo functions to DEGs
    based on embeddings to statistically validate these functional hypotheses. This approach
    facilitates gene–function association testing when predefined gene set annotations are unavailable
    and enables the statistical assessment of novel biological hypotheses inferred from LLMs.
